WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.367 --> 00:00:05.950
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:10.320
Welcome to "Cloud OnAir," live
webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.780
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.760
My name is Indranil Chakraborty.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.160
I'm the product manager
for Cloud IoT Core.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.247
And we also have--

00:00:18.247 --> 00:00:19.080
SAMRAT BAIRARIA: Hi.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.430
My name is Samrat Bairaria.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.580
I'm a technical program
manager at Google Cloud,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.119
focusing on IoT Core.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:26.910
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
And what Samrat and I

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.070
will be talking about
is Google Cloud IoT

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.960
solution and Cloud IoT Core.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.270
You can ask any questions
any time on the platform.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:37.770
And we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.070
So let's get started.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.180
As a starting point, we need
to clarify what we mean by IoT.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.890
The term is overloaded,
as you know.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.830
But let's stop and
think about it a bit.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.880
Imagine a city with
no congestion at all.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.820
Can IoT do that?

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:02.600
Imagine an airport
with no delays.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.840
Would IoT be able to play
a role in that as well?

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.830
Imagine the perfect energy
consumption every day

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.180
and every place.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.950
How would IoT make that happen?

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.930
As in health care,
as well, can you

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:23.990
imagine medicine that would
be tailored with each person?

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.270
Again, can IoT help
with that as well?

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.790
Like we see with games,
but now with physical toys,

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:36.320
these are the cues for IoT,
connecting the physical world

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.900
to the cloud, and
being able to get

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.200
a comprehensive view of
an ecosystem embedded

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.290
with those fantastic outcomes.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:45.260
IoT is all [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:46.100
Yes.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.160
But the reality is
that IoT is everywhere,

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:54.520
and is the key to a better
outcome for everyone.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:59.850
Now, it is estimated that 8.4
billion devices are connected

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.810
just in 2017, which
is more than 30%

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.880
higher as compared
to the previous year.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.660
We are in a fast accelerating
part of an exponential curve.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:15.880
A single jet engine from GE
generates about 500 gigabytes

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:17.570
of data per flight.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:22.360
Two jet engine per plane
means 1 terabyte of data.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.570
How many flights per day?

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.920
You see where I'm
going with this, right?

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.530
Pratt and Whitney's
geared turbofan

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.080
fitted with 5,000
sensors generate up

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.970
to 10 gigabytes of
data per second.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:43.810
A single twin engine aircraft
with an average 12 hour flight

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:48.574
time could produce up to
844 terabytes of data.

00:02:48.574 --> 00:02:49.990
To put it into
perspective, it was

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.060
estimated that
Facebook accumulated

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:57.520
around 600 terabytes of
data every day in 2014.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:03.790
But with more than
7,000 engines,

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:05.830
Pratt and Whitney
could potentially

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:09.490
download zettabytes of
data once all the engines

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:10.570
are in the field.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:13.840
Devices are much faster, and
they're constantly generating

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.660
a large amount of data.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.680
And we are at a time
where we are generating

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.240
more data than ever before.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.530
And not only are our devices
being connected and generating

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.870
an extremely large
volume of data,

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:31.250
it is happening virtually
in every industry.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:33.650
If we look at manufacturing
or industrial,

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:36.590
we are seeing impacts to
every aspect of business,

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:38.960
from connected devices
on the shop floor,

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:40.960
to connected products
in the field.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.056
From a paper machine,
you know that one--

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:44.930
and the one which makes
diapers, for example,

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:48.890
generates about 4 terabytes
of data per machine annually.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:51.230
Or perhaps a gas
or a steam turbine

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:55.400
generates a ton of
data annually as well.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.380
In fact, we have
partners who are already

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.070
delivering solutions
for manufacturing

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:02.990
built on the Google
Cloud Platform, which

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.600
we will talk about as well.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.700
We are also seeing IoT
solutions in health care,

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:12.200
whether it is personalized
medicine or asset tracking

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:12.920
in hospital.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:17.089
Someone like a company called
Dexcom, which continuously

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:20.000
monitors glucose
today, and are sending

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.820
10k compressed
[INAUDIBLE],, which

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:27.110
is expected to grow to about
1 terabyte of data per month.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:28.110
This is compressed size.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.170
The extracted data
will likely be

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:34.331
10x more than what it sends
from the glucose monitor.

00:04:34.331 --> 00:04:36.080
If we look at the
transportation industry,

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.954
for example, we're seeing
significant changes

00:04:37.954 --> 00:04:39.000
there as well.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.750
We're seeing applications
in commercial vehicles,

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:43.400
to vehicles and the suppliers.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.130
For example, we're seeing
the use of telematics

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.710
to improve customer
service by responding

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.560
to early signs of failure
in commercial vehicles,

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:55.000
and avoiding unexpected
breakdowns and towing.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.680
This use of telematics
and maps are, again,

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.160
generating a ton of data,
which can be then processed

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.979
to get meaningful
insights from the field.

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:06.770
If we look at consumer
products industries,

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:08.186
we're seeing a
number of use cases

00:05:08.186 --> 00:05:11.720
there as well for
marketing personalization.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:14.390
But recently, we want to deal
with a large toy manufacturer,

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:18.260
focused on reinventing their
business driving innovation

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:19.610
and disruption.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:20.870
So you get the point.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:24.200
When you think about these
large volume billions of devices

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.230
connecting to cloud, or
through the internet,

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:31.160
it results in generating
a massive amount of data

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:32.870
from all these
devices, which are

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:37.580
much larger than what humans
have been generating so far.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.440
So this is IoT data.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.260
And Google has been
dealing with this kind

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:43.962
of data for a long time.

00:05:43.962 --> 00:05:45.920
In fact, Google is one
of those companies which

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.420
has seven products which
has more than one billion

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.360
30 day active users.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.400
And all of these
devices and users

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:57.950
are generating a ton
of data in petabytes

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:00.630
or in exabytes every day.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:04.035
And what happens
when you collect

00:06:04.035 --> 00:06:07.520
that massive scale of data?

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:09.440
Well, you become a
master of big data.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.300
And you become a
data driven company.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:12.920
This is our strength.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:16.790
This is what Google brings to
customers that nobody else can.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:19.310
Mastering big data
has some benefits.

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:23.420
We can use the data
to get key insights,

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:27.810
we can build machine learning
models, and so on and so forth.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.890
It allows you to supercharge
innovation, and bring

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.070
new answers to hard problems.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:35.840
We have been working
on this for a decade.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:39.500
And we have used our
knowledge to help

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:44.060
our customers and our developers
in solving these big data

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:46.130
and machine learning
problems when you collect

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:48.780
this massive scale of data.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.550
We have worked in
the energy sector

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.380
with our data
center optimization.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:55.630
We have also worked in
many other fields as well.

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:00.350
When we are talking
about IoT, we

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:03.380
don't just focus
on one IoT product,

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.344
or a couple of IoT products.

00:07:05.344 --> 00:07:07.010
We really want to
drive the conversation

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:11.030
to a business outcome
enabled by Google Cloud IoT--

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.120
analyzing big data
in stream or batch,

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.030
and then applying machine
learning capabilities.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:19.850
It is together, Cloud
IoT as a solution,

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:22.940
and under the Cloud IoT
umbrella that customers

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:25.980
will be supercharged.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:29.960
So what does it mean when
we say a Cloud IoT solution?

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:33.690
Well, as we started talking
to our customers, partners,

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:38.750
and developers, there were
three challenges which appeared.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:41.270
And as Google, as
a company, we have

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:43.730
been dealing with these
challenges for a long time.

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:46.940
First is securely
connecting things.

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:51.050
Security applied to device
connectivity, and communication

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:52.070
and identity.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:54.770
Nobody wants their
devices to be hacked.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:58.220
And you've heard cases in the
past where this has happened.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:00.360
And you know of
the consequences.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:03.800
So how do you make sure that
all these devices, hundreds

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:05.390
of thousands,
millions or billions

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:08.480
of devices, which
are out in the field,

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.460
have the right
security patches, have

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:13.270
the right version
of their firmware,

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:18.080
and are very unlikely to
be hacked and compromised?

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:21.320
And how do you make sure that
all those devices can securely

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:25.880
connect to Google Cloud or
to other cloud platforms?

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:27.740
Second is scale.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:29.600
We're talking about
unprecedented data.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:34.520
As I just mentioned,
engines generate

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.039
gigabytes of data per day.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.380
And so when you think
about that level of scale,

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:42.020
how do you make sure
that you can ingest

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.690
the data at scale from
millions of billions

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:46.640
of these connected devices?

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:50.210
And you can store all this
data in a cost effective way.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:54.660
And you can analyze and process
it in a meaningful way as well.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:57.020
And third is
actionable insights.

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:00.150
Once you have this data,
what do you do with it?

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:02.830
At the end of the day, why
does a customer need IoT if he

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:05.220
cannot get any
meaningful insights?

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.470
And this is, again,
where we have

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:10.350
been working with our
consumer products and a number

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:14.760
of products to understand,
to analyze data

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:17.490
to get really meaningful
insights which

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:23.940
we think we can share with
the audience at large.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:26.780
So, as I mentioned,
we have been working

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:30.650
for a decade on large scale
infrastructure and machine

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:31.620
learning.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.980
And we have built our IoT
stack on the same technology

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:40.550
and tools that we use at Google,
for Google Search, for at least

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:44.290
two billion Android devices that
connect to Google every day.

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:53.250
And we have an
end-to-end solution

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:57.990
to derive intelligence in the
moment with Google Cloud IoT.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:02.010
Here is an
architecture of devices

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:05.190
that can connect to Google
Cloud via Cloud IoT Core, which

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:08.720
is a new service which
I will just mention.

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:11.800
And you can use our
downstream services,

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:16.140
such as Pub/Sub, Cloud Function,
DataFlow, Cloud BigQuery,

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:20.590
machine learning, and
other services for storage,

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:23.160
processing, analysis,
and using as machine

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:26.110
learning for predictive
insights as well.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:29.460
The great thing about our
Google Cloud IoT solution

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:32.220
is that you can
seamlessly move IoT data

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.050
across Google services.

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:36.450
And you can decide which
services suit you best

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.610
for your business
use case, and then

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:41.640
use it for specific use cases.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.910
You can ingest data
with Cloud IoT Core.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:47.610
You can distribute data
with Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.790
apply data transformation
with Cloud DataFlow,

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:56.340
and then store data with Cloud
BigQuery, or big cloud storage,

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:57.490
or BigTable.

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:03.240
You can perform ad hoc
analysis with Google BigQuery,

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:06.330
visualize data using Cloud
Data Studio or Datalab,

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:11.660
and then derive intelligence
using cloud machine learning.

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:13.990
So let's start with IoT Core.

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:16.152
What is Cloud IoT Core?

00:11:16.152 --> 00:11:18.640
So Cloud IoT Core
is a new service

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:20.200
which we announced last year.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:24.910
And we just announced a general
availability of this service.

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:26.770
It's a fully
managed service that

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:30.370
allows you to easily and
securely connect your IoT

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:35.730
devices to Google Cloud, and
then manage and ingest data

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:38.205
from millions of
global [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:38.205 --> 00:11:42.170
And we think you would benefit
from this service as well.

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:44.600
So a couple of key
benefits of Cloud IoT Core.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:50.480
As you know, Google is probably
the only non-ISP company

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:54.350
who has fiber optic
deployment across the Atlantic

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:56.930
and across the Pacific Ocean.

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:59.960
And we have a number
of points of presence

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.300
throughout the globe.

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:07.640
What this allows us to do is
to provide a single global end

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:11.390
point for your devices to
connect to Cloud IoT Core.

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:15.680
Whether your devices, or
your vehicle, or your asset

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:18.650
is in the US, or it's
somewhere in Asia, or somewhere

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:21.020
in Africa, you just
connect your device

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:22.580
to Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:27.110
And leveraging our global
networking infrastructure,

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:30.050
we will connect it to the
closest point of presence.

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:32.840
And from there, we'll connect
it to our closest data center.

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:37.282
So you don't have to worry about
picking which particular server

00:12:37.282 --> 00:12:38.240
you need to connect to.

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.550
You just connect to one global
endpoint for Google Cloud IoT

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:41.150
Core.

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:42.260
And we take it from there.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.270
Cloud IoT Core has
two key components.

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:53.630
One is what we call
Protocol Bridge.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:56.020
And second is the
Device Manager.

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:57.460
What's great about
Protocol Bridge

00:12:57.460 --> 00:13:03.100
is we realized that most
of the industrial devices

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:06.550
are using standard
protocols such as MQTT,

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:09.200
and even HTTP in many cases.

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:13.060
So with Cloud IoT Core, we have
native support for MQTT 3.1

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:14.140
and above.

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:17.590
And what's great
about this is, you

00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:19.900
can connect your
devices without worrying

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:25.810
about instantiating a resource,
or an infrastructure, or a VM.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:27.550
Whether you're
connecting one device,

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:30.850
or you're connecting 10,000,
or you're connecting millions

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:33.580
of devices as you get
ready for deployment,

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:36.250
you just connect your
device over MQTT or HTTP,

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:38.950
and we internally scale for you.

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:40.720
So we have automatic
load balancing

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:45.010
built in as part of Cloud
IoT Core managed service.

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:49.850
And since Cloud IoT Core
connects with and publishes

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:55.930
its data to Cloud
Pub/Sub, you can remotely

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:59.260
access all your device data
from a central location.

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:00.880
And you can monitor
all your devices

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:04.280
or your factories from a
central location that way.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:06.920
The second component of Cloud
IoT Core is Device Manager.

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:11.990
And this is where we allow
users and developers to remotely

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:17.220
manage, control, and
monitor their devices.

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:21.080
So, first of all, we
really care about security.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:25.670
And so we have a provision
to individually authenticate

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:27.290
each IoT devices.

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:30.120
We support asymmetry key
based authentication,

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:34.400
where the public key is
stored in with Cloud IoT Core,

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:37.490
and the private key is
stored with the device.

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:43.210
And we use the standard
[? jar ?] token

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:44.670
to sign with the private key.

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:47.330
And that's what we use for
individually authenticating

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:49.290
each device.

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:52.880
You can use our Device
Manager on Cloud IoT Core

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:56.450
to update configuration
of the devices,

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:58.370
and even control the devices.

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:01.040
You can, for example,
send firmware updates,

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:03.440
or new configuration,
or new settings

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:06.740
remotely from the
cloud to the device.

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:09.800
Your application just
needs to call an API.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:13.700
And Cloud IoT Core makes
sure that those new settings

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:18.450
get propagated to all the
devices as they come online.

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:21.530
We also have added
row level access

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:25.230
to different groups of devices.

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:30.740
So, for example, you might want
to grant only View level access

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:33.680
to certain groups of users.

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:39.200
Or you might want to grant just
Add or Delete level access.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:42.590
So there are a
variety of roles which

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:46.820
you can grant by using Cloud
IM on Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:50.360
And you might have heard
this term, digital twin.

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:53.210
And this is something which
is used a lot in the industry.

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:55.640
With Cloud IoT Core, it
essentially offers you

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:58.790
a digital twin, which is
a logical representation

00:15:58.790 --> 00:16:03.320
of your device on
the Google Cloud.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:07.460
We also have a rich set
of APIs and a UI console

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:10.350
for device deployment
and monitoring as well.

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:13.820
So this is, again, a
fully managed service

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:16.190
with a rich set of APIs
which allows you to securely

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:20.600
connect, manage, and ingest data
at scale from your IoT devices.

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:25.660
Recently we added a
new set of features

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:31.900
as well with our recent
beta and GA release.

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:35.230
You can bring your
own certificate

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:37.060
for additional security.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:40.180
And our users can now
bring their own device key,

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:42.840
which is signed by the
certificate authority

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:45.490
for device authentication.

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:47.780
Logical device representation,
I just talked about,

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:52.030
which is essentially a digital
twin on Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:54.670
And we've also added
support for HTTP in addition

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:57.060
to MQTT as well.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:17:00.440
Now, we understand
that many of you,

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:06.680
whether you're a developer, or
a device partner, or a customer,

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:09.680
before you go full
scale deployment,

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:11.900
you would want to build
some prototypes, some proof

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:16.400
of concept to
understand the ROI,

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:19.190
and to really understand
the impact of IoT.

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:24.140
So in our case, we have a
simple UI, User Interface,

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:28.490
for management of devices,
and monitoring those devices

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:29.400
as well.

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:35.120
So here's a screenshot of
our Google Cloud console,

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:37.870
where if you scroll
down on the left menu,

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:42.560
and you go to IoT
Core, you can start

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:45.890
sort of adding your devices, and
then monitoring them as well.

00:17:45.890 --> 00:17:49.280
So it's a very simple UI which
can really get you started.

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:52.100
With IoT Core we have
a notion of registry,

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:56.140
which essentially is a
collection of devices

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:58.090
within a specific region.

00:17:58.090 --> 00:18:01.100
So you can start by
creating a device registry.

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:03.595
And then the next step, once
you create a device registry--

00:18:03.595 --> 00:18:04.970
for example, here
we have created

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:07.190
a registry called
Weather Station--

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:12.200
once you've created a registry,
you can add devices to it.

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:15.490
And as you add devices, you
would specify the public key

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:16.575
for authentication.

00:18:16.575 --> 00:18:18.200
And there are a bunch
of other settings

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:21.520
which you can add to it as well.

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:23.860
Once you've added and
registered a device,

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:26.460
when the physical device
connects to Google Cloud IoT

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:30.940
Core, you would be able to see
the status of those devices

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:34.065
on the same UI console.

00:18:34.065 --> 00:18:36.220
And with this UI
console you're also

00:18:36.220 --> 00:18:39.700
able to not just monitor
the incoming data,

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:44.110
but you're also able to send
configuration or updates

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:46.860
to the device from
the UI as well.

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:49.570
So we've made sure that
we not only can't handle

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:53.050
a massive scale, but we also
have a simple UI for you

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:56.920
to get started quickly for
your initial proof of concept

00:18:56.920 --> 00:18:58.857
or prototypes.

00:19:01.660 --> 00:19:06.820
So once you've used Cloud
IoT Core for connecting

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:10.780
your devices in a secure
way, and then you manage them

00:19:10.780 --> 00:19:16.060
at scale remotely, you can
use Pub/Sub for ingestion

00:19:16.060 --> 00:19:18.100
and distribution of data.

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:22.750
The great thing about Pub/Sub
is that it has durable message

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:26.800
persistence, which means
Pub/Sub stores the data which is

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:29.690
published to it for seven days.

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:33.900
So what happens here is as the
devices connect to IoT Core,

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:36.670
and as they publish data, as
they start transmitting data,

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:39.580
IoT Core then publishes
data from all those devices

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:43.030
in a registry to
one Pub/Sub topic.

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:48.140
And Pub/Sub then keeps
the data for seven days.

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:50.950
So even if you lose
some connectivity,

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:53.620
you still have
persistence on that data.

00:19:53.620 --> 00:19:55.150
And as Pub/Sub is
a global service,

00:19:55.150 --> 00:19:57.280
you can use Pub/Sub
to remotely monitor

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:01.720
to have a central monitoring
system for all your globally

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:03.760
dispersed devices.

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:07.330
In addition, Pub/Sub
can be a great tool

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:11.780
for distributing data to
other downstream services,

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:15.640
such as Cloud Function,
DataFlow, or even

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:18.710
your own ETL pipeline.

00:20:18.710 --> 00:20:22.335
So you can build a
simple business logic.

00:20:22.335 --> 00:20:23.660
Let's take an example.

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:27.520
If you just want to monitor
the incoming temperature

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:29.320
data from your
temperature sensor,

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:32.980
and say every time it goes
above 80 degrees Fahrenheit

00:20:32.980 --> 00:20:35.230
you want to turn on the fan.

00:20:35.230 --> 00:20:38.770
What you can do here is you can
write a simple Cloud Function,

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:40.270
which subscribes to Pub/Sub.

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:43.720
And as it gets those
temperature readings

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:46.490
from the device that's
connected to Cloud IoT Core,

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:48.100
your logic will kick in.

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:51.610
And then you can send a
command back to Cloud IoT Core

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:53.620
to turn on the fan.

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:56.290
DataFlow can be used
for more complex event

00:20:56.290 --> 00:21:00.670
processing, similar to how
you use Cloud Function.

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:02.650
And here you can have a window.

00:21:02.650 --> 00:21:05.260
And you can say, I want to
compute the median temperature

00:21:05.260 --> 00:21:08.080
over a 10 minute window
or a 10 second window.

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:12.580
This is great for complex
data processing as well.

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:14.380
And what Pub/Sub
allows you to do

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:18.220
is it allows you to
abstract and separate

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:21.760
your upstream devices from
your downstream application.

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:23.590
So you can continue
to make changes

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:26.830
on your downstream
application without meaning

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:31.300
to update the library or the
firmware of your devices.

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:33.760
So Cloud IoT Core in
conjunction with Pub/Sub

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:36.370
provides you a great
set of services

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:41.800
for securely connecting
your devices at scale,

00:21:41.800 --> 00:21:44.590
ingesting data at scale, and
then distributing this data

00:21:44.590 --> 00:21:47.200
to downstream services
for storage, processing,

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:49.930
and analysis.

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:52.560
So that was Cloud IoT Core and
how you can build a solution

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:55.080
with Google Cloud for IoT.

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:57.255
And now I'm going to hand
it off to Samrat, who

00:21:57.255 --> 00:21:58.939
is going to give you a demo.

00:21:58.939 --> 00:22:00.480
SAMRAT BAIRARIA:
Thank you, Indranil.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:03.000
So for this demo which
we are about to show

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:05.160
you simulates an
asset tracking company

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:08.402
which can have multiple devices
or sensors around the globe.

00:22:08.402 --> 00:22:08.902
I can do it.

00:22:08.902 --> 00:22:10.350
We can just go back quickly.

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:19.130
So we wanted to
showcase how we can

00:22:19.130 --> 00:22:21.630
build an end-to-end application
using Google Cloud IoT

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:23.810
Core and other Google
Cloud Services,

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:26.570
like DataFlow,
BigQuery, App Engine,

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:29.400
and visualize data on a
dashboard in real time.

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:32.350
I wanted to quickly go over
how the back end is set up.

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:34.460
IoT Core is designed
specifically

00:22:34.460 --> 00:22:37.160
to securely connect,
manage, and ingest data

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:39.350
from globally dispersed devices.

00:22:39.350 --> 00:22:42.440
We have set up Pub/Sub
topics and device registries

00:22:42.440 --> 00:22:44.160
to connect the devices.

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:47.690
Once connected, they send
the telemetry data via MQTT

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:48.930
to the IoT Core endpoint.

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:55.760
As raw data is arriving
into Pub/Sub topics,

00:22:55.760 --> 00:23:00.830
we then use DataFlow, which
subscribes to Pub/Sub topics,

00:23:00.830 --> 00:23:02.060
set up earlier.

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:04.310
Cloud DataFlow is a
fully managed service

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:07.520
for transforming and
enriching data streaming in.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:10.310
This could be real
time or batch data.

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:12.290
The DataFlow pipeline
then dumps the data

00:23:12.290 --> 00:23:15.860
into BigQuery, which
is our data warehouse.

00:23:15.860 --> 00:23:17.840
BigQuery also auto scales.

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:19.400
And set up is easy.

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:25.600
All we need to do is specify
our data schema to get started.

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:28.810
So from there, we also have
some Cloud Functions set up,

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:32.180
which also check the value of
the data against Cloud SQL.

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:34.090
If a certain
threshold is crossed,

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:36.320
we can then trigger the
Cloud Function routine

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:38.220
based on alert required.

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:41.470
Current threshold setups
are defined for weather,

00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:43.700
temperature, and geofence.

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:47.800
We are also using Cloud Data
Store to store geofencing data.

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:51.320
And in the end, we're tying
all of this information using

00:23:51.320 --> 00:23:52.680
App Engine.

00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:54.990
In particular, App Engine Flex.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:57.900
And we are also using Google
Maps API to visualize the data

00:23:57.900 --> 00:23:59.980
points on the dashboard.

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:01.820
We are also calling
some shipping APIs

00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:06.590
to know the shipping routes
a certain ship might take.

00:24:06.590 --> 00:24:07.940
Now, switching over to the demo.

00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:21.840
So as you can see here,
we have built a dashboard

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:25.710
where we can see all devices
and sensors a typical asset

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:27.570
tracking company might have.

00:24:27.570 --> 00:24:29.730
We have the following
items on screen.

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:32.900
We have ships, which
are transferring goods

00:24:32.900 --> 00:24:34.170
around the globe.

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:35.940
And then we have
large containers,

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:41.370
which are carrying goods from
dockyards into warehouses.

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:42.960
And then we have
trucks out here,

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:45.480
which are delivery trucks
which are transferring data

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:47.580
from the warehouses
to a customer,

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:51.790
or to a certain
asset on the ground.

00:24:51.790 --> 00:24:53.350
One thing to note
here is, no matter

00:24:53.350 --> 00:24:56.710
where the devices are
located in the world,

00:24:56.710 --> 00:25:01.360
you need to not change which
endpoint the data is sent to.

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:08.080
So you can have
devices in the US,

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:13.140
in Australia, or even in China.

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:15.420
You, the developer, do not
need to specify an endpoint

00:25:15.420 --> 00:25:16.940
where the data has to go.

00:25:16.940 --> 00:25:19.470
Google front end will take
care of routing the packet

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:21.330
to the closest location.

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:22.830
Let's drill down a
little bit deeper

00:25:22.830 --> 00:25:26.310
on what kind of information
the devices are sending us.

00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:28.740
So in this case, we have a ship.

00:25:28.740 --> 00:25:31.990
And the ships have a
bunch of sensors on them.

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:34.890
And if you can see, this is
a passenger fleet out here.

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:36.860
We have a passenger
fleet ship out here.

00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:39.150
And we're calling
shipping APIs to see

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:40.590
what is the flag of the ship?

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:43.860
And then we have a bunch of
sensors on the ship itself,

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:46.620
which is streaming
data using IoT Core.

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:50.540
We can see temperature readings.

00:25:50.540 --> 00:25:52.420
We can see the course
the ship is taking,

00:25:52.420 --> 00:25:55.270
as well as the wind speed
the ship is encountering.

00:25:55.270 --> 00:25:57.400
All this information
is being streamed

00:25:57.400 --> 00:25:58.870
through IoT Core in real time.

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:00.850
And we can visualize
this information

00:26:00.850 --> 00:26:04.280
on the dashboard
running our App Engine.

00:26:04.280 --> 00:26:08.380
Let's take another example here
of a truck which is en route

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:12.050
or which is on a delivery
route to a customer.

00:26:12.050 --> 00:26:14.300
So for this truck,
again, we have a bunch

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:16.790
of sensors on this truck as
it's traveling from point A

00:26:16.790 --> 00:26:21.080
to point B. We want to track,
what is the equipment status?

00:26:21.080 --> 00:26:23.840
Is it full, or has
data been unloaded?

00:26:23.840 --> 00:26:26.270
We want to make sure
of the door status.

00:26:26.270 --> 00:26:29.030
This is for security purposes to
see if someone opened the cargo

00:26:29.030 --> 00:26:30.650
door during transit.

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:34.410
We also have ambient temperature
within the cargo hold.

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:38.060
And we also have the
device battery levels

00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:39.860
for each device
that is on board.

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:47.060
As the truck is making its
journey across from point A

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:50.240
to point B, it's streaming
data to us in real time.

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:52.970
And we can visualize
this on screen.

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:56.160
We can also interact with
the devices on these trucks.

00:26:56.160 --> 00:26:58.160
For example, we want
to set the threshold

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:01.820
of what is the perfect
temperature the cargo

00:27:01.820 --> 00:27:02.780
hold should have.

00:27:02.780 --> 00:27:04.110
So let's say in this case--

00:27:04.110 --> 00:27:05.401
let's take it a little extreme.

00:27:05.401 --> 00:27:08.300
Let's take it we want it
to be one degree Celsius.

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:16.259
Once you set that, the IoT Core
communicates back to the device

00:27:16.259 --> 00:27:17.800
and tells it, this
is a new threshold

00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:19.390
it needs to act against.

00:27:19.390 --> 00:27:21.310
We also use Cloud
Functions in the back end

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:23.710
to trigger if certain
thresholds are crossed.

00:27:23.710 --> 00:27:26.670
So if you can see on screen, as
the threshold has been crossed,

00:27:26.670 --> 00:27:29.170
it is having a alerts
generated on screen.

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:33.040
You can also see the
history of all the data

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:35.350
that this device
has been streaming.

00:27:35.350 --> 00:27:37.690
All this information is
stored into BigQuery.

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:41.830
We are passing the data from
the device, to IoT Core,

00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:43.720
to DataFlow, and
then to BigQuery.

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:46.150
And then we are acquiring
it using the App Engine

00:27:46.150 --> 00:27:48.830
application.

00:27:48.830 --> 00:27:52.340
You can also interact with other
APIs like the Google Maps API,

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:55.560
and then make this whole
internal application work.

00:27:55.560 --> 00:27:57.410
Let's take an example
out here where

00:27:57.410 --> 00:27:59.570
we want to separate a geofence.

00:27:59.570 --> 00:28:01.291
And if a device
crosses the geofence

00:28:01.291 --> 00:28:02.540
we want some kind of an alert.

00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:21.780
As you see, if the device is
not in a particular geofence,

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:24.870
we have a notification
on screen.

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:27.289
So we wanted to show the
robustness of IoT Core,

00:28:27.289 --> 00:28:28.830
and how it interacts
with the various

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:31.230
of the subsystems
underneath, and how fast

00:28:31.230 --> 00:28:33.010
this whole application works.

00:28:33.010 --> 00:28:35.730
You can also see
historical alerts

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:39.810
that the devices have been
generating and sending it

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:40.620
through IoT Core.

00:28:50.490 --> 00:28:53.670
So like Indranil
said earlier, it

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:56.280
doesn't matter if you have a few
hundred devices, or thousands

00:28:56.280 --> 00:28:57.654
or devices, or a
million devices.

00:28:57.654 --> 00:29:00.079
We scale automatically for you.

00:29:00.079 --> 00:29:02.370
You can seamlessly interact
with other Google products,

00:29:02.370 --> 00:29:05.160
like Google Maps API, and data
store, based on data streaming,

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:07.850
into IoT Core.

00:29:07.850 --> 00:29:09.500
Hopefully this
small demo was able

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:11.330
to demonstrate the
robustness of IoT Core,

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:14.330
and how you can use it for
your own business logic,

00:29:14.330 --> 00:29:16.400
or for your own
personal projects.

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:19.290
Thank you for your time,
and over to you, Indranil.

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:21.930
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
Thank you, Samrat.

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:23.545
Let's go back to the slides.

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:37.060
So, as Samrat gave you a
demo of asset tracking--

00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:38.920
and what was great
in this demo was

00:29:38.920 --> 00:29:42.730
you could see real time
the movement of the assets.

00:29:42.730 --> 00:29:46.270
And whether it's in the US
or a different location,

00:29:46.270 --> 00:29:52.060
we can still provide the
same real time tracking

00:29:52.060 --> 00:29:53.980
regardless of the location.

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:58.420
And it uses IoT
Core Google Geo APIs

00:29:58.420 --> 00:30:01.090
and a bunch of other
services where all of this

00:30:01.090 --> 00:30:04.070
comes together for
a complete solution.

00:30:04.070 --> 00:30:07.870
So the point I want
to make here is,

00:30:07.870 --> 00:30:10.880
Google Cloud IoT is really a
platform for end-to-end IoT

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:12.370
data processing.

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:17.830
So for ingestions,
connection, and management

00:30:17.830 --> 00:30:21.947
of your IoT device data, we
have IoT Core Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:30:21.947 --> 00:30:23.530
We also have Android
Things, which I'm

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:25.480
going to talk about in a bit.

00:30:25.480 --> 00:30:29.470
For processing, cleaning,
and storing, in a cost

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:32.740
effective way for this
massive amount of data,

00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:36.460
we have DataFlow function, Cloud
Functions, BigTable, Spanner,

00:30:36.460 --> 00:30:37.870
and even GCS.

00:30:37.870 --> 00:30:40.030
And, finally, to analyze
and visualize, and then

00:30:40.030 --> 00:30:43.240
predict outcomes, we have
BigQuery, Cloud Datalab,

00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:45.730
Machine Learning
Engine, Data Studio.

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:48.960
And you can even use Cloud
Functions and DataFlow.

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:52.420
So Google Cloud
really has all the set

00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:55.866
of services which you need to
build an end-to-end solution,

00:30:55.866 --> 00:30:57.490
and build some
compelling applications,

00:30:57.490 --> 00:30:59.350
just the way Samrat
just showed you

00:30:59.350 --> 00:31:00.730
about real time asset tracking.

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:05.770
I want to touch upon
Android Things, which

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:11.920
is our version of an operating
system for the IoT devices.

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:14.890
Android Things essentially
is Android for IoT.

00:31:14.890 --> 00:31:16.840
And whats great
about Android Things

00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:21.440
is it gives you
three key benefits.

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:24.590
One is, since it's built on
Android, it's highly secure.

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:28.690
It's a secured boot
operating system.

00:31:28.690 --> 00:31:31.420
And if you have a
gateway or a device which

00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:35.980
runs Android Things, it's
very hard for that device

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:37.390
to be compromised.

00:31:37.390 --> 00:31:41.620
Second is it's fully
managed by Google.

00:31:41.620 --> 00:31:45.390
When you have millions
or billions of IoT

00:31:45.390 --> 00:31:47.800
devices globally
dispersed, you want

00:31:47.800 --> 00:31:51.170
to make sure that all those
devices have the right firmware

00:31:51.170 --> 00:31:54.130
version, have the
right security patches,

00:31:54.130 --> 00:31:57.150
and have the same version
across all the devices.

00:31:57.150 --> 00:32:01.000
And just the way we do it
with our Android mobile phones

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:04.920
and Chromebooks,
Android Things also

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:08.320
ensures that all
your fleet of devices

00:32:08.320 --> 00:32:12.040
will have the same version of
operating system and security

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:13.220
patches as well.

00:32:13.220 --> 00:32:17.660
So that's great for a
large enterprise, and even

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:20.080
a medium sized
enterprise customer.

00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:23.070
And finally,
Android Things comes

00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:27.320
with support for some
of our Google services,

00:32:27.320 --> 00:32:29.110
such as TensorFlow
and machine learning.

00:32:29.110 --> 00:32:32.110
So you can imagine if
you have a camera which

00:32:32.110 --> 00:32:37.510
runs Android Things and you have
a cloud machine learning model,

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:42.520
or a TensorFlow
model to detect faces

00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:48.200
of employees of your company,
training of the model

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:49.270
can happen in cloud.

00:32:49.270 --> 00:32:52.310
The inference can still
happen locally on the camera,

00:32:52.310 --> 00:32:55.540
using Android Things
and its set of APIs.

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:58.540
So it really makes it
easy for you to build

00:32:58.540 --> 00:32:59.970
compelling applications.

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:02.550
And the benefit of Android
Things, the related benefit

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:05.170
is since it's Android,
you are tapping

00:33:05.170 --> 00:33:08.780
into the Android application
developer ecosystem as well.

00:33:08.780 --> 00:33:13.870
So it's really easy to build
edge applications for edge

00:33:13.870 --> 00:33:15.770
compute as well.

00:33:15.770 --> 00:33:18.620
So with Android Things,
it's secure manageability,

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:24.280
secure boot, and APIs
for machine learning.

00:33:24.280 --> 00:33:28.850
And it works seamlessly
with Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:33:28.850 --> 00:33:31.600
We believe we have a
pretty compelling solution

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:33.370
for not just on
the cloud aspect,

00:33:33.370 --> 00:33:34.870
but also on the
device aspect so you

00:33:34.870 --> 00:33:39.130
can build an end-to-end solution
for your business specific use

00:33:39.130 --> 00:33:41.710
cases.

00:33:41.710 --> 00:33:49.400
We also understand that
in order for our platform

00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:53.429
to be more sort of
useful, we can't just

00:33:53.429 --> 00:33:54.470
do everything on our own.

00:33:54.470 --> 00:33:58.870
So we've been working hard to
build our partner ecosystem,

00:33:58.870 --> 00:34:02.900
both on the device side, as
well as on the application side.

00:34:02.900 --> 00:34:05.650
So we have a number
of device partners,

00:34:05.650 --> 00:34:12.010
such as Intel, Wireless,
NXP, Arm, Marvell.

00:34:12.010 --> 00:34:16.449
There are a large number
of device partners

00:34:16.449 --> 00:34:21.159
who have IoT devices
which work seamlessly

00:34:21.159 --> 00:34:22.340
with Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:34:22.340 --> 00:34:26.219
So if you're using any
of our partner devices,

00:34:26.219 --> 00:34:28.929
it'll work seamlessly with
Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:34:28.929 --> 00:34:34.350
We also have a number of
SIs and application partners

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:36.489
as part of the
ecosystem who can help

00:34:36.489 --> 00:34:41.800
you build your specific
application for your IoT use

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:48.310
cases, companies such as
Mnubo, Agosto, Losant, SOTEC.

00:34:48.310 --> 00:34:51.280
All of these
partners can help you

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:54.429
build specific IoT
applications using Google Cloud

00:34:54.429 --> 00:34:55.660
Platform and Cloud IoT Core.

00:34:55.660 --> 00:34:57.910
So we are very, very excited
about our growing partner

00:34:57.910 --> 00:34:58.814
ecosystem.

00:34:58.814 --> 00:34:59.980
And this is just a snapshot.

00:34:59.980 --> 00:35:05.520
We have a lot more on
the website as well.

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:07.670
So why Cloud IoT?

00:35:07.670 --> 00:35:12.650
And I think I just want to
summarize the reason why

00:35:12.650 --> 00:35:16.070
we think you, as a
developer, as a partner,

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:18.770
or as a customer should use
Google Cloud IoT for your IoT

00:35:18.770 --> 00:35:20.020
application.

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:24.320
One, Cloud IoT Core lets
developers and enterprise

00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:27.680
easily connect your millions
of devices, which are globally

00:35:27.680 --> 00:35:30.530
dispersed, through
the protocol endpoint,

00:35:30.530 --> 00:35:33.020
against a global endpoint,
with less hassle,

00:35:33.020 --> 00:35:37.830
and no worries about scale.

00:35:37.830 --> 00:35:39.970
We take care of
automatic scaling.

00:35:39.970 --> 00:35:42.470
And that's great for
applications and developers

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:44.660
so that you can focus on
your business application

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:48.470
and not worry about the
underlying infrastructure.

00:35:48.470 --> 00:35:52.580
Second is, as part of
Google Cloud IoT platform,

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:56.390
you're not just collecting
your device data,

00:35:56.390 --> 00:35:58.670
but connecting your
global device network

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:00.440
on intelligent cloud.

00:36:00.440 --> 00:36:06.440
Google Cloud machine learning
and big data innovations

00:36:06.440 --> 00:36:09.850
helps you to make
sense of the IoT data.

00:36:09.850 --> 00:36:13.100
You can perform ad hoc analysis,
as I mentioned, with BigQuery.

00:36:13.100 --> 00:36:16.160
You can visualize
data with Data Studio.

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:20.630
And you can derive meaningful,
intelligent insights

00:36:20.630 --> 00:36:23.450
using Cloud Machine Learning.

00:36:23.450 --> 00:36:28.320
And third is, Cloud IoT Core
is a service architecture.

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:30.660
It's a fully managed service.

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:34.100
So you don't have to instantiate
a separate instance of Cloud

00:36:34.100 --> 00:36:35.120
IoT Core.

00:36:35.120 --> 00:36:38.435
You will just connect your
devices to Cloud IoT Core,

00:36:38.435 --> 00:36:40.590
and off you go with
your application.

00:36:40.590 --> 00:36:44.270
So whether you start
with few devices

00:36:44.270 --> 00:36:47.990
as you embark on your journey
with the proof of concept,

00:36:47.990 --> 00:36:49.920
to when you're
ready for deployment

00:36:49.920 --> 00:36:53.930
and you want to deploy millions,
or hundreds of thousands,

00:36:53.930 --> 00:36:58.820
or billions of devices,
it's the same application.

00:36:58.820 --> 00:37:00.320
It's the same service
which you just

00:37:00.320 --> 00:37:01.880
connected to without
worrying about

00:37:01.880 --> 00:37:04.290
the underlying infrastructure.

00:37:04.290 --> 00:37:08.360
Fourth is, given
our global network,

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:12.200
we can really offer
minimal latency.

00:37:12.200 --> 00:37:16.370
And especially in the case
of IoT, every millisecond,

00:37:16.370 --> 00:37:17.840
every second matters.

00:37:17.840 --> 00:37:19.410
And latency is critical.

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:21.990
And Google ensures
that your devices

00:37:21.990 --> 00:37:25.280
are delivered with the
lowest latency using

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:28.340
our global networking
infrastructure.

00:37:28.340 --> 00:37:31.290
The highest quality
of private networks

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:33.850
that connects our regional
locations and data

00:37:33.850 --> 00:37:36.320
centers to more than
100 global network

00:37:36.320 --> 00:37:39.770
points of presence close to
your device and your users.

00:37:39.770 --> 00:37:42.800
This means that the
device will always

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:45.560
connect to the closest
point of presence

00:37:45.560 --> 00:37:49.370
and benefit from our
global network backbone

00:37:49.370 --> 00:37:51.450
with the lowest latency.

00:37:51.450 --> 00:37:53.550
And finally, Cloud
IoT core works

00:37:53.550 --> 00:37:55.620
seamlessly with millions
of Android Things

00:37:55.620 --> 00:37:59.160
devices, and devices from
leading hardware makers,

00:37:59.160 --> 00:38:05.850
manufacturers, such as Intel,
NXP, Microchip, Marvell, Sierra

00:38:05.850 --> 00:38:07.804
Wireless, and others.

00:38:07.804 --> 00:38:09.720
And the Android Things
device operating system

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:12.060
is updated and passed by Google.

00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:14.040
As data is generated
in the sensor,

00:38:14.040 --> 00:38:17.700
it seamlessly moves into
Google Cloud for processing,

00:38:17.700 --> 00:38:19.727
analyzing, and integration.

00:38:22.530 --> 00:38:28.230
So that was an
overview and a demo

00:38:28.230 --> 00:38:31.341
for building IoT
solutions on Google Cloud.

00:38:31.341 --> 00:38:32.340
Thank you for your time.

00:38:32.340 --> 00:38:36.550
And stay tuned for live Q&amp;A.
We will be back in less than a

00:38:36.550 --> 00:38:37.050
minute.

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:19.080
Thank you everyone
for the questions

00:40:19.080 --> 00:40:21.250
that we received
from the audience.

00:40:21.250 --> 00:40:23.640
Samrat and I are going to
walk you through and try

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:25.390
to answer as many as we can.

00:40:25.390 --> 00:40:26.350
So let's get started.

00:40:26.350 --> 00:40:29.040
So the first
question we had was,

00:40:29.040 --> 00:40:32.970
what are some of the
advantages of MQTT over HTTP?

00:40:32.970 --> 00:40:35.320
So there are a
couple of advantages.

00:40:35.320 --> 00:40:38.050
First of all, what we find
is that in the industry,

00:40:38.050 --> 00:40:41.490
many of the devices
are already using MQTT.

00:40:41.490 --> 00:40:45.630
And MQTT, as you know, is
a relatively new format

00:40:45.630 --> 00:40:47.400
which was introduced
in, I think,

00:40:47.400 --> 00:40:49.320
towards the end of the 1990s.

00:40:49.320 --> 00:40:53.520
The key benefits are, with
MQTT, it's built over TCP.

00:40:53.520 --> 00:40:57.720
So you can use TLS and achieve
the same level of security

00:40:57.720 --> 00:40:59.880
as you do with HTTP.

00:40:59.880 --> 00:41:02.554
In addition, with
MQTT, the payload size,

00:41:02.554 --> 00:41:04.220
the bandwidth consumption
is much lower.

00:41:07.850 --> 00:41:11.310
You can send binary
data over MQTT.

00:41:11.310 --> 00:41:13.030
And that saves you
a lot in bandwidth.

00:41:13.030 --> 00:41:14.580
So that is one of
the key reasons why

00:41:14.580 --> 00:41:15.951
many of those devices use MQTT.

00:41:15.951 --> 00:41:18.200
SAMRAT BAIRARIA: And you
don't need much storage space

00:41:18.200 --> 00:41:18.869
with MQTT.

00:41:18.869 --> 00:41:20.910
So you can have a smaller
footprint for a device.

00:41:20.910 --> 00:41:21.270
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
That's right.

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:23.440
You don't need
much storage space.

00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:29.040
And then second is MQTT has this
topic structure which really

00:41:29.040 --> 00:41:33.840
works well for devices,
as devices publish data

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:35.580
on a particular
topic, and you have

00:41:35.580 --> 00:41:39.194
applications subscribing to
those topics to collect data.

00:41:39.194 --> 00:41:41.610
And you can also use it for
device to device communication

00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:42.380
as well.

00:41:42.380 --> 00:41:45.820
So I think those are the two
key benefits of MQTT over HTTP.

00:41:45.820 --> 00:41:47.527
SAMRAT BAIRARIA: [INAUDIBLE].

00:41:47.527 --> 00:41:49.110
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
Second question.

00:41:49.110 --> 00:41:52.600
What formats are
supported for payload?

00:41:52.600 --> 00:41:57.060
So the way we look
at IoT Core is,

00:41:57.060 --> 00:41:59.790
we've taken a set of
data agnostic and format

00:41:59.790 --> 00:42:01.350
agnostic approach.

00:42:01.350 --> 00:42:04.080
At the same time,
our recommendation

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:06.885
is, you can use
JSON as a format.

00:42:06.885 --> 00:42:10.110
You can also use Protobuf.

00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:14.110
If bandwidth is
critical for you,

00:42:14.110 --> 00:42:16.200
and you want to
save every bit, you

00:42:16.200 --> 00:42:18.870
can use Protobuf as well
to send binary data.

00:42:21.410 --> 00:42:24.380
Third question is, do
I have to create keys

00:42:24.380 --> 00:42:26.890
for devices on the cloud?

00:42:26.890 --> 00:42:28.074
You want to take that?

00:42:28.074 --> 00:42:28.990
SAMRAT BAIRARIA: Yeah.

00:42:28.990 --> 00:42:31.610
Well, you can use keys, or
you can also use certificates.

00:42:31.610 --> 00:42:34.010
We have a bring your own
certificate mechanism

00:42:34.010 --> 00:42:36.450
where you can generate
your own certificate.

00:42:36.450 --> 00:42:38.930
Give us the public
certificate on IoT Core.

00:42:38.930 --> 00:42:40.730
And you can have
your device assigned

00:42:40.730 --> 00:42:42.790
with your private certificate.

00:42:42.790 --> 00:42:45.830
So you can choose whatever
path you want to take.

00:42:45.830 --> 00:42:49.010
You can create your
keys on Cloud IoT Core.

00:42:49.010 --> 00:42:51.910
You can do it using Open
SSL on your machine.

00:42:51.910 --> 00:42:53.770
Or you can get your
own certificates.

00:42:53.770 --> 00:42:54.895
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY: Yeah.

00:42:54.895 --> 00:42:57.480
And I think, if I
understand it right,

00:42:57.480 --> 00:43:00.510
you really want to create
virtual devices on cloud

00:43:00.510 --> 00:43:03.630
for the purpose of testing.

00:43:03.630 --> 00:43:05.160
And the short answer is, yes.

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:08.180
Even if you are
creating virtual devices

00:43:08.180 --> 00:43:10.460
to connect to
Cloud IoT Core, you

00:43:10.460 --> 00:43:13.410
have to use private
keys and public keys,

00:43:13.410 --> 00:43:16.640
a private-public key combination
to connect to Cloud IoT Core.

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:18.240
Otherwise it will
reject connection.

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:22.670
And on our website, there
are very clear instructions

00:43:22.670 --> 00:43:27.420
and samples which will
help you to generate keys,

00:43:27.420 --> 00:43:28.970
whether it's for
a virtual device,

00:43:28.970 --> 00:43:31.950
or even for a physical device.

00:43:31.950 --> 00:43:36.990
Then the final question is, how
can I use multiple MQTT topics?

00:43:36.990 --> 00:43:39.880
So there are two ways to
address this question.

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:45.060
One is, if you're trying to use
multiple topics on the device

00:43:45.060 --> 00:43:50.990
side, today we said we had
two parent level topics.

00:43:50.990 --> 00:43:55.680
So one for sending telemetry,
which are telemetry events,

00:43:55.680 --> 00:43:59.520
and one for the device
to publish its own state

00:43:59.520 --> 00:44:00.570
information.

00:44:00.570 --> 00:44:07.420
And for telemetry, the
topic is slash events.

00:44:07.420 --> 00:44:09.070
Devices slash device
ID, slash events.

00:44:09.070 --> 00:44:10.920
But you can create subfolders.

00:44:10.920 --> 00:44:13.565
So if you want to
send temperature

00:44:13.565 --> 00:44:15.440
on a separate topic,
you can say slash event,

00:44:15.440 --> 00:44:18.600
slash temperature, or slash
event, slash pressure.

00:44:18.600 --> 00:44:23.330
And interesting enough, just
now in our GA release we also

00:44:23.330 --> 00:44:25.170
have a new feature
which will allow you to

00:44:25.170 --> 00:44:28.980
map these different MQTT
topics to a different Pub/Sub

00:44:28.980 --> 00:44:30.780
topic on Google Cloud.

00:44:30.780 --> 00:44:34.190
So you can map the temperature
topic to temperature Pub/Sub,

00:44:34.190 --> 00:44:36.457
to [INAUDIBLE] the topic,
and then so on and so forth.

00:44:36.457 --> 00:44:38.040
So you can really
use multiple topics,

00:44:38.040 --> 00:44:41.430
both on the device side, as
well as on the cloud side.

00:44:41.430 --> 00:44:42.210
Great.

00:44:42.210 --> 00:44:44.220
